 ## Overview
-Solid Voxelization based on Octree converts a polygon mesh (specifically OBJ format) into a hierarchical octree representation to determine the solid, empty, and distance-to-surface status of leaf nodes [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from fixed-grid voxelization by using adaptive spatial subdivision, allowing for efficient representation of sparse scenes or high-resolution details where a uniform grid would be prohibitively large [S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Solid Voxelization via Ray Casting](6829-solid-voxelization-via-ray-casting.md), which casts rays per pixel to find intervals, this method recursively partitions space and assigns triangles to nodes, using ray sampling only for ambiguous internal empty leaves [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Solid Voxelization based on Octree converts a polygon mesh (specifically OBJ format) into a hierarchical octree representation to determine the solid, empty, and distance-to-surface status of leaf nodes [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from fixed-grid voxelization by using adaptive spatial subdivision, allowing for efficient representation of sparse scenes or high-resolution details where a uniform grid would be prohibitively large [S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Solid Voxelization via Ray Casting](6829-solid-voxelization-via-ray-casting.md), which casts rays per pixel to find intervals, this method recursively partitions space and assigns triangles to nodes, using ray sampling only for ambiguous internal empty leaves [S2, unofficial].
 
